  "query_preview": "..\nOvernight an MCS (Mesoscale Convective System) moved through\nCochise and Santa Cruz counties, with its current position over\nSanta Cruz county. Behind this system, there will be subsidence\nleft in its wake, delaying and/or limiting today's afternoon and\nevening storms in these areas. Outside of these areas, the best\nchances for storms are in the White Mountains. \n\nSaturday looks to be shaping up to be an increase in\nthunderstorm activity, with SPC introducing an area of marginal\nrisk for severe winds for areas east of Tucson. The high\npressure moves from overhead to northward, introducing SW flow \naloft. The 31/00Z HREF is showing an area of probability of 10% \nwind gusts 50kts/58mph along the AZ/NM border in Graham, \nGreenlee and Cochise counties. With the placement of these \nprobabilities, blowing dust can be an issue along I-10 from San \nSimon to Willcox and in Cochise county. Isolated areas of flash \nflooding due to locally heavy rainfall and/or training \nthunderstorms over the same areas is possible, but not the main \nimpact expected Saturday.\n\nThe high pressure continues to meander generally near northern\nArizona, keeping Southeast Arizona under SW flow into early next\nweek, keeping daily chances for showers and thunderstorms each\nafternoon and evening.\n\nOutside of storm chances, the Extreme Heat continues through the\nweekend with temperatures 10-15 degrees above normal with near\nto record breaking heat each day. Continue to practice heat\nsafety by staying indoors (with AC when possible) during peak\nheating, staying hydrated, consider moving plans indoors,\nknowing the signs for heat illness, and checking in on\nvulnerable populations over the coming days.",
